00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:14.160
um so i want to talk today about kind of moving&nbsp;
towards a culture of reproducibility and when i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.640
talk about reproducibility i'm primarily focusing&nbsp;
on what people often refer to as computational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.560
reproducibility um you know this is ping's uh kind&nbsp;
of spectrum of reproducibility on on the one hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.800
you have kind of you know like full replication&nbsp;
like a new study new data um i'm going to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.280
kind of just before that the idea of you know&nbsp;
kind of sharing everything that goes into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.600
analysis and simply being able to reproduce&nbsp;
the results that one finds now now obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.600
you know in the end we want to be able to&nbsp;
generalize out to new data and and new you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.920
know new analysis code as well and i'll talk some&nbsp;
about that but the the point here i want to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.280
here is i'm really focusing on reproducibility as&nbsp;
sort of um not quite all the way to replication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:03.200
but simply taking a a completed study and you know&nbsp;
showing that we can reproduce the results now um&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.320
you know a lot of my thinking is inspired by this&nbsp;
