00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.920
stanford he was really one of the pioneers of kind&nbsp;
of reproducible data science who said an article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.960
about a computational result is advertising&nbsp;
not scholarship the actual scholarship is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:25.680
the full software environment code and data that&nbsp;
produce the result so why is this a problem well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.000
you know first in many different domains data&nbsp;
analysis today is highly complex and offers an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.120
extreme degree of flexibility in the way that one&nbsp;
can do one analyses and it's generally impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.440
to reproduce a result just using the publication&nbsp;
and even with the code and the data it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.600
different difficult to reproduce the results so&nbsp;
i want to sort of talk through an example of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.240
from a study that i did a few years ago&nbsp;
this was a study where i had scanned myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.600
repeatedly over the course of a year and a half&nbsp;
collected you know blood data did rna sequencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.400
um and then we published a paper where we&nbsp;
tried to build a reproducible analysis workflow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.840
and we did this using a technology called&nbsp;
virtual machines which is basically building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.840
sort of a a little computer inside a computer&nbsp;
that should look exactly the same way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.320
anybody who runs it and so we gave this out&nbsp;
and basically said hey if you run this thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.880
you install it in your computer and&nbsp;
run it you know it'll take a few hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.280
and ultimately you'll have all the same results&nbsp;
that we used in the that we report in the paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:38.000
so i get an email maybe a year later hi i'm&nbsp;
an undergraduate student at johns hopkins i'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.280
trying to you know reproduce your results i've run&nbsp;
into some trouble reproducing the rna seek results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.680
they're supposed to be 63 coexpression modules&nbsp;
but when i run the scripts i only get 60. i reran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.280
the analysis with the vm and the real result is&nbsp;
60. um essentially the same code is run on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:01.600
same data but arrives at two different results&nbsp;
what might be going on and it turns out that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.520
you look at the version of the r package that we&nbsp;
used to do that analysis in the paper it was 1.47&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.120
by the time you know he went to run&nbsp;
the analysis it was version 1.51 and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.560
turns out that our virtual machine pulled&nbsp;
the latest version of the code and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:24.880
little change from 1.747 to 1.51 resulted in&nbsp;
differences in the results of the analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.640
even though we had this like fully reproducible&nbsp;
in theory uh analysis platform we also know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.600
you know small differences can actually have&nbsp;
impact on analysis results this is some work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:44.240
from tristan guitard a few years ago showing&nbsp;
that if you run free surfer on uh on structural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.840
imaging data you can get different results either&nbsp;
between different linux versions or even between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.120
different software builds which they ultimately&nbsp;
track down to particular numeric libraries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:02.880
um so that's that's one sort of set of concerns&nbsp;
around uh you know the platform dependence and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:09.680
version dependence of results another has to do&nbsp;
with software errors so um you know much of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:16.320
we do as scientists is really amateur software&nbsp;
