00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:58.480
five papers because there was a piece of code that&nbsp;
they used they'd actually gotten from someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.160
that ended up causing a sine flip that caused&nbsp;
the wrong uh handedness of the protein structures

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.040
and a particular concern is you know what some of&nbsp;
us have started calling bug hacking which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:20.960
idea that um when you know bugs aren't neutral&nbsp;
in terms of you know our likelihood of finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.680
them right if we have a bug that disconfirms&nbsp;
our predictions it gives us results that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.240
quote unquote don't like we're much more&nbsp;
likely to dig in and sort of find that bug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.400
than if we have a bug that gives us the&nbsp;
results that we're expecting or sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:41.040
gives us results that are better than they&nbsp;
actually should be so i'm going to argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.960
that we need to develop a culture of computational&nbsp;
reproducibility that everything that we do needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:53.840
to be sort of focused on how we can do science&nbsp;
that's computationally reproducible and i'm i'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.720
going to lay this out in terms of um an idea that&nbsp;
that was inspired by reading uh kent beck's work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.160
on extreme programming where he talks about&nbsp;
a culture of of you know software development&nbsp;&nbsp;

